# Trivy findings that are accepted, each with the reason and where to verify it.
#
# This file suppresses a finding from a scanner, which is a thing worth doing
# rarely and never quietly. Every entry names what the rule checks, why it does
# not hold here, and what would make it a real problem again.

# DS-0002 — "Specify at least 1 USER command in Dockerfile with non-root user"
#
# Applies to: deployments/railway/nats/Dockerfile
#
# The rule is a static one: it reads the Dockerfile and sees no USER, so it
# concludes the container runs as root. For this image that conclusion is wrong.
#
# A mounted volume arrives owned by root whatever the image declares. A NATS
# container that simply declares USER finds its JetStream store unwritable and
# exits with "storage directory is not writable"; the common workaround is to
# run the message bus as root, which is the outcome this rule exists to prevent.
#
# entrypoint.sh instead fixes ownership while still privileged and then hands
# the server to uid 10001 with su-exec, which replaces the process rather than
# forking — so the server runs unprivileged and as PID 1, receiving signals
# directly. The root phase lasts microseconds and never runs the network server.
# The script also handles the case where the platform already set a non-root
# user, in which case it drops nothing and simply starts.
#
# This becomes a real finding again if entrypoint.sh stops calling su-exec, or
# if the exec'd uid becomes 0. Verify with:
#
#   docker exec griefer-nats-1 ps -o pid,user,comm
#
# PID 1 must be nats-server running as "griefer". Do NOT check this with
# `docker exec griefer-nats-1 id`: exec starts a new process under the image's
# default user and answers root, which says nothing about the server that is
# already running and reads as though the drop never happened.
DS-0002
